% EU AI Act triage: the classification of one AI system, the role of each
% operator, and the obligation map, under Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (the
% Artificial Intelligence Act) as amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (the
% "Digital Omnibus on AI").
%
% Source: the consolidated text 02024R1689-20260727 of the Publications
% Office of the European Union (CELEX 02024R1689-20260727), retrieved on
% 3 November 2026. Each rule names the article, paragraph and point that it
% encodes. The consolidated text is a documentation tool with no legal effect;
% the authentic text is the one published in the Official Journal.
%
% This program is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. It is not
% legal advice. It is a triage, not a conformity assessment: a suspected
% prohibited practice, a high-risk system and general-purpose AI go to a
% human for a full analysis (query escalation).
%
% WHAT IT IS FOR. It replaces the determinations of the Mike OSS workflow "EU
% AI Act Triage" (mike-workflows, eu-ai-governance-pack, version 1.1.0): an
% assistant reads the documents of a matter and writes the facts about the
% system as sentences of the templates below; this program decides, and
% explains each answer with the articles it used. The plan is
% lpsPlus/docs/strategy/MikeOSS_plan.md.
%
% HOW IT WAS MADE. Written by hand, with an AI agent, from the consolidated
% text. The ten scenarios are fictitious systems; their expected answers were
% worked out from the text before the program was run.
%
% HOW TO READ IT.
% - Each case names the system that it triages and the date of the triage:
%   "TalentSift is triaged on 2026-11-03." Every date of application is
%   compared with that date, so the same case gives a different obligation
%   map on a different day.
% - What a case does not state is false, except where the regulation needs
%   the fact to decide. There, a missing fact is a GAP (query gaps): for
%   example, an Annex III system that does not say whether it profiles
%   persons cannot use the Article 6(3) derogation, and the gap says why.
%   Where a fact decides against the system, the program asks for it to be
%   stated: "PayCalc does not infer how to generate its outputs".
% - Adaptiveness after deployment is something an AI system MAY exhibit
%   (Article 3(1)); it is not a condition, so no template asks for it.
% - "high-risk" and every citation are written inside quotation marks,
%   because a template cannot contain a hyphen or a bracket.

the target language is: prolog.

scenario facts require provenance.

the templates are:

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- the case
*a system* is triaged on *a date*; undefined.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- scope (Article 2)
*a system* is used exclusively for military or defence or national security purposes; undefined.
*a system* is developed and put into service for the sole purpose of scientific research and development; undefined.
*a system* is in the scope of the regulation.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 1: an AI system (Article 3(1))
*a system* is a machine based system; undefined.
*a system* is designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy; undefined.
*a system* infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs; undefined.
*a system* does not infer how to generate its outputs; undefined.
the outputs of *a system* can influence physical or virtual environments; undefined.
*a system* is not a machine based system; undefined.
*a system* is an AI system.
*a system* is not an AI system.
the triage of *a system* continues.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- the operators (Article 3(3) to (8), Article 25)
*an operator* places *a system* on the market under its own name; undefined.
*an operator* puts *a system* into service under its own name; undefined.
*an operator* uses *a system* under its own authority; undefined.
*an operator* uses *a system* only in a purely personal non professional activity; undefined.
*an operator* is established in the Union; undefined.
*an operator* places on the Union market *a system* that bears the name or trademark of an operator established in a third country; undefined.
*an operator* makes *a system* available on the Union market as a distributor; undefined.
*an operator* holds a written mandate from the provider of *a system*; undefined.
*an operator* puts its name or trademark on *a system*; undefined.
*an operator* makes a substantial modification to *a system*; undefined.
*an operator* modifies the intended purpose of *a system*; undefined.
*a system* was not classified as high risk before its intended purpose was modified; undefined.
*an operator* is the initial provider of *a system*.
*an operator* is considered the provider of *a system* under *a provision*.
*an operator* is a provider of *a system*.
*an operator* is a deployer of *a system*.
*an operator* is an importer of *a system*.
*an operator* is a distributor of *a system*.
*an operator* is an authorised representative for *a system*.
*an operator* has the role *a role* for *a system*.
*an operator* is an operator of *a system*.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 2: prohibited practices (Article 5)
*a system* deploys subliminal or purposefully manipulative or deceptive techniques that materially distort the behaviour of persons and cause or are likely to cause significant harm; undefined.
*a system* exploits vulnerabilities of persons due to their age or disability or social or economic situation and materially distorts their behaviour with significant harm; undefined.
*a system* evaluates or classifies natural persons over time on their social behaviour or personal characteristics with a social score that leads to unjustified or unrelated detrimental treatment; undefined.
*a system* assesses or predicts the risk of a natural person committing a criminal offence based solely on profiling or on personality traits; undefined.
*a system* only supports a human assessment of the involvement of a person in a criminal activity that is based on objective and verifiable facts; undefined.
*a system* creates or expands facial recognition databases through the untargeted scraping of facial images from the internet or CCTV footage; undefined.
*a system* infers emotions of natural persons in the workplace or in education institutions; undefined.
*a system* is intended to be put in place for medical or safety reasons; undefined.
*a system* categorises natural persons individually on their biometric data to deduce their race or political opinions or trade union membership or religious or philosophical beliefs or sex life or sexual orientation; undefined.
*a system* is used for real time remote biometric identification in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement; undefined.
the use of *a system* is strictly necessary for the search for victims or the prevention of an imminent threat or the localisation of a suspect of a serious offence; undefined.
generating *a material* is the intended purpose of *a system*; undefined.
*a system* makes generating *a material* a reasonably foreseeable and reproducible outcome; undefined.
*a system* has adequate safeguards that reliably prevent generating *a material*; undefined.
*an operator* uses *a system* to generate *a material*; undefined.
*a material* is the material of *a provision*.
*a system* involves the practice *a practice* prohibited by *a provision*.
*an operator* is subject to the prohibition of *a provision* for *a system*.
*a system* involves a prohibited practice.
the prohibition of *a provision* applies from *a date*.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 3: high risk (Article 6, Annexes I and III)
*a system* is itself a product covered by *an act*; undefined.
*a system* is a component of a product covered by *an act*; undefined.
*a system* fulfils a safety function for the product that it is a component of; undefined.
the failure or malfunctioning of *a system* would endanger the health and safety of persons or property; undefined.
*a system* is used solely for non safety related purposes such as user assistance or optimisation; undefined.
*a system* must undergo a third party conformity assessment under *an act*; undefined.
the third party conformity assessment of *a system* is required solely due to risks other than risks to health and safety; undefined.
*an act* is listed in *a provision*.
*an act* is in Section *a section* of Annex I.
*a system* is a safety component of a product covered by *an act*.
*a system* is a product or safety component under *an act* that needs a third party conformity assessment.
*a system* is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 1 because of *an act*.

*a system* is intended to be used for *a use*; undefined.
*a system* is used for the purpose of detecting financial fraud; undefined.
*a use* is listed in *a provision*.
*a system* is used for a use listed in *a provision*.
*a system* is intended to perform a narrow procedural task; undefined.
*a system* is intended to improve the result of a previously completed human activity; undefined.
*a system* is intended to detect decision making patterns or deviations from them without replacing or influencing the human assessment; undefined.
*a system* is intended to perform a preparatory task to an assessment relevant for the use cases of Annex III; undefined.
*a system* performs profiling of natural persons; undefined.
*a system* does not perform profiling of natural persons; undefined.
*a system* meets a condition of Article 6 paragraph 3 under *a provision*.
*a system* is not high risk under Article 6 paragraph 3 because of *a provision*.
*a system* is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of *a provision*.
*a system* is high risk.
*a system* is classified as *a class* on the basis of *a provision*.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 4: general purpose AI models (Chapter V)
*an operator* places the general purpose AI model *a model* on the market; undefined.
*a system* is based on the general purpose AI model *a model*; undefined.
the cumulative training computation of *a model* is *a number* floating point operations; undefined.
the Commission designates *a model* as a general purpose AI model with systemic risk; undefined.
*a model* is released under a free and open source licence with its parameters publicly available; undefined.
*a model* was placed on the market on *a date*; undefined.
*an operator* is the provider of the general purpose AI model *a model* of *a system*.
*a model* has systemic risk under *a provision*.
the date of application of Chapter V to *a model* is *a date*.
general purpose AI is in play for *a system*.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 5: transparency (Article 50)
*a system* is intended to interact directly with natural persons; undefined.
it is obvious to the natural persons concerned that they interact with *a system*; undefined.
*a system* generates synthetic *a kind* content; undefined.
*a system* only performs an assistive function for standard editing or does not substantially alter the input data; undefined.
*a system* is authorised by law to detect or prevent or investigate or prosecute criminal offences; undefined.
*a system* is an emotion recognition system or a biometric categorisation system; undefined.
*a system* generates or manipulates image or audio or video content that is a deep fake; undefined.
*an operator* publishes text generated by *a system* to inform the public on matters of public interest; undefined.
the text that *an operator* publishes from *a system* has undergone human review or editorial control under its editorial responsibility; undefined.
*a system* was first placed on the market or put into service on *a date*; undefined.
the date of application of Article 50 paragraph 2 to *a system* is *a date*.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 7: the obligation map (Articles 4, 6, 25 to 27, 50, 53, 55, 111, 113)
*an operator* is a body governed by public law; undefined.
*an operator* is a private entity providing public services; undefined.
*an operator* is a private entity that does not provide public services; undefined.
*an operator* is a public authority; undefined.
*an operator* has a data protection impact assessment for *a system*; undefined.
the date of application of Chapter III to *a system* is *a date*.
*an obligation* applies to *an operator* for *a system* from *a date*.
*an obligation* already applies to *an operator* for *a system*.
*an obligation* applies to *an operator* for *a system* only from *a date* that is in *a number* days.
the prohibition of *a provision* already applies to *an operator* for *a system*.
the prohibition of *a provision* applies to *an operator* for *a system* only from *a date* that is in *a number* days.
*an operator* must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for *a system*.
*an operator* may reuse its data protection impact assessment in the fundamental rights impact assessment for *a system*.
the fundamental rights impact assessment for *a system* is *a verdict*.
*a system* is subject to Chapter III only after a significant change in its design from *a date*.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 8: the triage card
the triage of *a system* has the gap *a gap*.
*a system* goes to a human for a full analysis because *a reason*.

the knowledge base eu_ai_act_triage includes:

% ================================================================ Article 2: scope
section applicability is:

rule scope with provenance the AI Act at "Article 2(3), second subparagraph, and Article 2(6)":
a system is in the scope of the regulation
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and it is not the case that
        the system is used exclusively for military or defence or national security purposes
    and it is not the case that
        the system is developed and put into service for the sole purpose of scientific research and development.

% ================================================================ step 1: Article 3(1)
rule ai_system with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)",
        confer "a machine-based system that is designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy and that may exhibit adaptiveness after deployment, and that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs":
a system is an AI system
    if the system is a machine based system
    and the system is designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy
    and the system infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs
    and the outputs of the system can influence physical or virtual environments
    and it is not the case that
        the system is not an AI system.

rule not_ai_system_inference with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
a system is not an AI system
    if the system does not infer how to generate its outputs.

rule not_ai_system_machine with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
a system is not an AI system
    if the system is not a machine based system.

% The triage goes on only for an AI system in scope that involves no
% prohibited practice: the workflow stops at a suspected prohibited practice
% and sends it to a human.
the triage of a system continues
    if the system is in the scope of the regulation
    and the system is an AI system
    and it is not the case that
        the system involves a prohibited practice.

% ================================================================ step 6: the operators
section roles is:

rule provider_by_placing with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(3)":
an operator is the initial provider of a system
    if the operator places the system on the market under its own name.

rule provider_by_service with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(3)":
an operator is the initial provider of a system
    if the operator puts the system into service under its own name.

rule provider with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(3) and Article 25(2), first subparagraph",
        confer "the provider that initially placed the AI system on the market or put it into service shall no longer be considered to be a provider of that specific AI system":
an operator is a provider of a system
    if the operator is the initial provider of the system
    and it is not the case that
        an other operator is considered the provider of the system under a provision.

rule provider_requalified with provenance the AI Act at "Article 25(1)":
an operator is a provider of a system
    if the operator is considered the provider of the system under a provision.

rule requalified_name with provenance the AI Act at "Article 25(1), point (a)":
an operator is considered the provider of a system under "Article 25(1), point (a)"
    if the operator puts its name or trademark on the system
    and an other operator is the initial provider of the system
    and the system is high risk.

rule requalified_modification with provenance the AI Act at "Article 25(1), point (b)":
an operator is considered the provider of a system under "Article 25(1), point (b)"
    if the operator makes a substantial modification to the system
    and an other operator is the initial provider of the system
    and the system is high risk.

rule requalified_purpose with provenance the AI Act at "Article 25(1), point (c)":
an operator is considered the provider of a system under "Article 25(1), point (c)"
    if the operator modifies the intended purpose of the system
    and an other operator is the initial provider of the system
    and the system was not classified as high risk before its intended purpose was modified
    and the system is high risk.

rule deployer with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(4) and Article 2(10)":
an operator is a deployer of a system
    if the operator uses the system under its own authority
    and it is not the case that
        the operator uses the system only in a purely personal non professional activity.

rule importer with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(6)":
an operator is an importer of a system
    if the operator is established in the Union
    and the operator places on the Union market the system that bears the name or trademark of an operator established in a third country.

rule distributor with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(7)":
an operator is a distributor of a system
    if the operator makes the system available on the Union market as a distributor
    and it is not the case that
        the operator is a provider of the system
    and it is not the case that
        the operator is an importer of the system.

rule authorised_representative with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(5)":
an operator is an authorised representative for a system
    if the operator is established in the Union
    and the operator holds a written mandate from the provider of the system.

an operator has the role "provider" for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a provider of the system.
an operator has the role "deployer" for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a deployer of the system.
an operator has the role "importer" for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is an importer of the system.
an operator has the role "distributor" for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a distributor of the system.
an operator has the role "authorised representative" for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is an authorised representative for the system.

rule operator with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(8)":
an operator is an operator of a system
    if the operator has the role a role for the system.

% ================================================================ step 2: Article 5
section question is:

rule practice_a with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (a)":
a system involves the practice "manipulation causing significant harm" prohibited by "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (a)"
    if the system deploys subliminal or purposefully manipulative or deceptive techniques that materially distort the behaviour of persons and cause or are likely to cause significant harm.

rule practice_b with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (b)":
a system involves the practice "exploitation of vulnerabilities" prohibited by "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (b)"
    if the system exploits vulnerabilities of persons due to their age or disability or social or economic situation and materially distorts their behaviour with significant harm.

rule practice_c with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (c)":
a system involves the practice "social scoring" prohibited by "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (c)"
    if the system evaluates or classifies natural persons over time on their social behaviour or personal characteristics with a social score that leads to unjustified or unrelated detrimental treatment.

rule practice_d with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (d)",
        confer "this prohibition shall not apply to AI systems used to support the human assessment of the involvement of a person in a criminal activity, which is already based on objective and verifiable facts":
a system involves the practice "crime prediction based solely on profiling" prohibited by "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (d)"
    if the system assesses or predicts the risk of a natural person committing a criminal offence based solely on profiling or on personality traits
    and it is not the case that
        the system only supports a human assessment of the involvement of a person in a criminal activity that is based on objective and verifiable facts.

rule practice_e with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (e)":
a system involves the practice "untargeted scraping of facial images" prohibited by "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (e)"
    if the system creates or expands facial recognition databases through the untargeted scraping of facial images from the internet or CCTV footage.

rule practice_f with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (f)",
        confer "except where the use of the AI system is intended to be put in place or into the market for medical or safety reasons":
a system involves the practice "emotion recognition in the workplace or in education" prohibited by "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (f)"
    if the system infers emotions of natural persons in the workplace or in education institutions
    and it is not the case that
        the system is intended to be put in place for medical or safety reasons.

rule practice_g with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (g)":
a system involves the practice "biometric categorisation by sensitive attributes" prohibited by "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (g)"
    if the system categorises natural persons individually on their biometric data to deduce their race or political opinions or trade union membership or religious or philosophical beliefs or sex life or sexual orientation.

rule practice_h with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (h)":
a system involves the practice "real-time remote biometric identification for law enforcement" prohibited by "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (h)"
    if the system is used for real time remote biometric identification in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement
    and it is not the case that
        the use of the system is strictly necessary for the search for victims or the prevention of an imminent threat or the localisation of a suspect of a serious offence.

% Points (ba) and (bb), added by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744. Placing on the
% market or putting into service is prohibited only where the generation is
% the intended purpose, or a foreseeable and reproducible outcome without
% adequate safeguards (Article 5(1a), point (a)); use only where the deployer
% uses the system for that purpose (Article 5(1a), point (b)). Article 5(1b)
% (a manipulation that does not increase exposure is not a manipulation) is
% left to the person who states the facts.
intimate imagery made without consent is the material of "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (ba)".
child sexual abuse material is the material of "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (bb)".

rule material_by_purpose with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1a), point (a)(i)":
a system involves the practice a material prohibited by a provision
    if the material is the material of the provision
    and generating the material is the intended purpose of the system.

rule material_by_design with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1a), point (a)(ii)":
a system involves the practice a material prohibited by a provision
    if the material is the material of the provision
    and the system makes generating the material a reasonably foreseeable and reproducible outcome
    and it is not the case that
        the system has adequate safeguards that reliably prevent generating the material.

rule material_by_use with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1a), point (b)":
a system involves the practice a material prohibited by a provision
    if the material is the material of the provision
    and an operator uses the system to generate the material.

rule prohibited with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1)":
a system involves a prohibited practice
    if the system involves the practice a practice prohibited by a provision.

% Who is bound: the provider places the system on the market or puts it
% into service; the deployer uses it. For points (ba) and (bb) the deployer
% is bound only when it uses the system for that purpose (Article 5(1a)(b)).
rule prohibition_provider with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph":
an operator is subject to the prohibition of a provision for a system
    if the system involves the practice a practice prohibited by the provision
    and the operator is a provider of the system
    and it is not the case that
        the provision is "Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (h)".

rule prohibition_deployer with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1), first subparagraph":
an operator is subject to the prohibition of a provision for a system
    if the system involves the practice a practice prohibited by the provision
    and the operator is a deployer of the system
    and it is not the case that
        the practice is the material of the provision.

rule prohibition_deployer_material with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1a), point (b)":
an operator is subject to the prohibition of a provision for a system
    if the material is the material of the provision
    and the operator uses the system to generate the material
    and the operator is a deployer of the system.

rule prohibition_dates with provenance the AI Act at "Article 113, third paragraph, point (a)",
        confer "Chapters I and II shall apply from 2 February 2025, with the exception of Article 5(1), first subparagraph, points (ba) and (bb), and Article 5(1a) and (1b) which shall apply from 2 December 2026":
the prohibition of a provision applies from a date
    if a material is the material of the provision
    and the date is 2026-12-02
    otherwise the date is 2025-02-02.

% ================================================================ step 3: Article 6(1) and Annex I
the toys directive is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 2 (Directive 2009/48/EC, toys)".
the recreational craft directive is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 3 (Directive 2013/53/EU, recreational craft)".
the lifts directive is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 4 (Directive 2014/33/EU, lifts)".
the explosive atmospheres directive is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 5 (Directive 2014/34/EU, equipment for explosive atmospheres)".
the radio equipment directive is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 6 (Directive 2014/53/EU, radio equipment)".
the pressure equipment directive is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 7 (Directive 2014/68/EU, pressure equipment)".
the cableway installations regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 8 (Regulation (EU) 2016/424, cableways)".
the personal protective equipment regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 9 (Regulation (EU) 2016/425, personal protective equipment)".
the gas appliances regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 10 (Regulation (EU) 2016/426, gas appliances)".
the medical devices regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 11 (Regulation (EU) 2017/745, medical devices)".
the in vitro diagnostic medical devices regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section A, point 12 (Regulation (EU) 2017/746, in vitro diagnostic medical devices)".
the civil aviation security regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 13 (Regulation (EC) No 300/2008, civil aviation security)".
the regulation on two wheel and three wheel vehicles is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 14 (Regulation (EU) No 168/2013)".
the agricultural and forestry vehicles regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 15 (Regulation (EU) No 167/2013)".
the marine equipment directive is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 16 (Directive 2014/90/EU, marine equipment)".
the rail interoperability directive is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 17 (Directive (EU) 2016/797, rail system)".
the motor vehicles approval regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 18 (Regulation (EU) 2018/858, motor vehicles)".
the vehicle general safety regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 19 (Regulation (EU) 2019/2144, vehicle general safety)".
the civil aviation regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 20 (Regulation (EU) 2018/1139, civil aviation)".
the machinery regulation is listed in "Annex I, Section B, point 21 (Regulation (EU) 2023/1230, machinery)".

the toys directive is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the recreational craft directive is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the lifts directive is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the explosive atmospheres directive is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the radio equipment directive is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the pressure equipment directive is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the cableway installations regulation is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the personal protective equipment regulation is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the gas appliances regulation is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the medical devices regulation is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the in vitro diagnostic medical devices regulation is in Section "A" of Annex I.
the civil aviation security regulation is in Section "B" of Annex I.
the regulation on two wheel and three wheel vehicles is in Section "B" of Annex I.
the agricultural and forestry vehicles regulation is in Section "B" of Annex I.
the marine equipment directive is in Section "B" of Annex I.
the rail interoperability directive is in Section "B" of Annex I.
the motor vehicles approval regulation is in Section "B" of Annex I.
the vehicle general safety regulation is in Section "B" of Annex I.
the civil aviation regulation is in Section "B" of Annex I.
the machinery regulation is in Section "B" of Annex I.

% A safety component (Article 3(14)) fulfils a safety function, or its failure
% endangers health and safety. A component used solely for user assistance,
% optimisation, efficiency, automation, convenience or quality control is not
% one (Article 6(1a)), unless its failure would endanger health and safety
% (Article 6(1b)).
rule safety_component_by_failure with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(14) and Article 6(1b)":
a system is a safety component of a product covered by an act
    if the system is a component of a product covered by the act
    and the failure or malfunctioning of the system would endanger the health and safety of persons or property.

rule safety_component_by_function with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(14) and Article 6(1a)",
        confer "AI systems that are solely used for non-safety related aspects of user assistance, performance optimisation, service efficiency, automation or convenience or quality control shall not qualify as safety components":
a system is a safety component of a product covered by an act
    if the system is a component of a product covered by the act
    and the system fulfils a safety function for the product that it is a component of
    and it is not the case that
        the system is used solely for non safety related purposes such as user assistance or optimisation.

rule annex_i_product with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(1), points (a) and (b), and Article 6(1c)":
a system is a product or safety component under an act that needs a third party conformity assessment
    if the system is itself a product covered by the act
    and the system must undergo a third party conformity assessment under the act
    and it is not the case that
        the third party conformity assessment of the system is required solely due to risks other than risks to health and safety.

rule annex_i_component with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(1), points (a) and (b), and Article 6(1c)":
a system is a product or safety component under an act that needs a third party conformity assessment
    if the system is a safety component of a product covered by the act
    and the system must undergo a third party conformity assessment under the act
    and it is not the case that
        the third party conformity assessment of the system is required solely due to risks other than risks to health and safety.

rule high_risk_annex_i with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(1)":
a system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 1 because of an act
    if the system is an AI system
    and the system is a product or safety component under the act that needs a third party conformity assessment
    and the act is listed in a provision.

% ================================================================ step 3: Article 6(2), Annex III and the Article 6(3) derogation
the remote biometric identification of natural persons is listed in "Annex III, point 1(a)".
the biometric categorisation of natural persons according to sensitive or protected attributes is listed in "Annex III, point 1(b)".
the emotion recognition of natural persons is listed in "Annex III, point 1(c)".
the use as a safety component in the management and operation of critical digital infrastructure, road traffic or the supply of water, gas, heating or electricity is listed in "Annex III, point 2".
the determination of access or admission to educational and vocational training institutions is listed in "Annex III, point 3(a)".
the evaluation of learning outcomes is listed in "Annex III, point 3(b)".
the assessment of the level of education that an individual will receive or can access is listed in "Annex III, point 3(c)".
the monitoring and detection of prohibited behaviour of students during tests is listed in "Annex III, point 3(d)".
the recruitment or selection of natural persons is listed in "Annex III, point 4(a)".
the decisions on work relationships, the allocation of tasks or the monitoring and evaluation of workers is listed in "Annex III, point 4(b)".
the evaluation of eligibility for essential public assistance benefits and services by or for public authorities is listed in "Annex III, point 5(a)".
the evaluation of the creditworthiness of natural persons or the establishment of their credit score is listed in "Annex III, point 5(b)".
the risk assessment and pricing of natural persons for life and health insurance is listed in "Annex III, point 5(c)".
the evaluation and classification of emergency calls, the dispatch of emergency first response services or emergency patient triage is listed in "Annex III, point 5(d)".
the assessment for law enforcement of the risk of a natural person becoming a victim of criminal offences is listed in "Annex III, point 6(a)".
the use for law enforcement as a polygraph or similar tool is listed in "Annex III, point 6(b)".
the evaluation for law enforcement of the reliability of evidence is listed in "Annex III, point 6(c)".
the assessment for law enforcement of the risk of a natural person offending or re-offending is listed in "Annex III, point 6(d)".
the profiling of natural persons for the detection, investigation or prosecution of criminal offences is listed in "Annex III, point 6(e)".
the use for migration, asylum or border control as a polygraph or similar tool is listed in "Annex III, point 7(a)".
the assessment of a security, irregular migration or health risk posed by a person entering a Member State is listed in "Annex III, point 7(b)".
the examination of applications for asylum, visa or residence permits is listed in "Annex III, point 7(c)".
the detection, recognition or identification of natural persons for migration, asylum or border control is listed in "Annex III, point 7(d)".
the assistance of a judicial authority in researching and interpreting facts and the law and in applying the law is listed in "Annex III, point 8(a)".
the influencing of the outcome of an election or referendum or of the voting behaviour of natural persons is listed in "Annex III, point 8(b)".

rule annex_iii_use with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(2) and Annex III":
a system is used for a use listed in a provision
    if the system is intended to be used for a use
    and the use is listed in the provision
    and it is not the case that
        the provision is "Annex III, point 5(b)"
        and the system is used for the purpose of detecting financial fraud.

rule condition_a with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(3), second subparagraph, point (a)":
a system meets a condition of Article 6 paragraph 3 under "Article 6(3), second subparagraph, point (a)"
    if the system is intended to perform a narrow procedural task.
rule condition_b with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(3), second subparagraph, point (b)":
a system meets a condition of Article 6 paragraph 3 under "Article 6(3), second subparagraph, point (b)"
    if the system is intended to improve the result of a previously completed human activity.
rule condition_c with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(3), second subparagraph, point (c)":
a system meets a condition of Article 6 paragraph 3 under "Article 6(3), second subparagraph, point (c)"
    if the system is intended to detect decision making patterns or deviations from them without replacing or influencing the human assessment.
rule condition_d with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(3), second subparagraph, point (d)":
a system meets a condition of Article 6 paragraph 3 under "Article 6(3), second subparagraph, point (d)"
    if the system is intended to perform a preparatory task to an assessment relevant for the use cases of Annex III.

% The derogation needs the case to STATE that the system does not profile:
% "an AI system referred to in Annex III shall always be considered to be
% high-risk where the AI system performs profiling of natural persons", and
% the provider who relies on the derogation must document its assessment
% (Article 6(4)). Without that statement the derogation does not apply, and
% the gap says so.
rule derogation with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(3), first and third subparagraphs",
        confer "Notwithstanding the first subparagraph, an AI system referred to in Annex III shall always be considered to be high-risk where the AI system performs profiling of natural persons":
a system is not high risk under Article 6 paragraph 3 because of a provision
    if the system is used for a use listed in a listing
    and the system meets a condition of Article 6 paragraph 3 under the provision
    and the system does not perform profiling of natural persons
    and it is not the case that
        the system performs profiling of natural persons.

rule high_risk_annex_iii with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(2) and Article 6(3)":
a system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of a provision
    if the system is an AI system
    and the system is used for a use listed in the provision
    and it is not the case that
        the system is not high risk under Article 6 paragraph 3 because of a condition.

a system is high risk
    if the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 1 because of an act.
a system is high risk
    if the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of a provision.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- the classification rows of the triage card
rule class_not_ai with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
a system is classified as "not an AI system" on the basis of "Article 3(1)"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and the system is not an AI system.

rule class_ai with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
a system is classified as "an AI system" on the basis of "Article 3(1)"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and the system is an AI system.

rule class_open with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
a system is classified as "possibly an AI system: the facts leave gaps" on the basis of "Article 3(1)"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and it is not the case that
        the system is an AI system
    and it is not the case that
        the system is not an AI system.

rule class_out_of_scope with provenance the AI Act at "Article 2":
a system is classified as "outside the scope of the regulation" on the basis of "Article 2(3) or Article 2(6)"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and it is not the case that
        the system is in the scope of the regulation.

rule class_prohibited with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1)":
a system is classified as "a suspected prohibited practice" on the basis of a provision
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and the system is an AI system
    and the system involves the practice a practice prohibited by the provision.

rule class_high_risk_annex_i with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(1) and Annex I":
a system is classified as "high-risk under Article 6(1)" on the basis of a provision
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 1 because of an act
    and the act is listed in the provision.

rule class_high_risk_annex_iii with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(2) and Annex III":
a system is classified as "high-risk under Article 6(2)" on the basis of a provision
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of the provision.

rule class_derogation with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(3)":
a system is classified as "not high-risk under the Article 6(3) derogation" on the basis of a provision
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is not high risk under Article 6 paragraph 3 because of the provision
    and it is not the case that
        the system is high risk.

rule class_not_safety_component with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(1a)":
a system is classified as "not high-risk: not a safety component" on the basis of "Article 6(1a)"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is a component of a product covered by an act
    and it is not the case that
        the system is a safety component of the product covered by the act
    and it is not the case that
        the system is high risk.

rule class_not_high_risk with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6 and Annexes I and III":
a system is classified as "not high-risk" on the basis of "Article 6 and Annexes I and III"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and it is not the case that
        the system is high risk
    and it is not the case that
        the system is not high risk under Article 6 paragraph 3 because of a provision
    and it is not the case that
        the system is a component of a product covered by an act.

rule class_systemic_risk with provenance the AI Act at "Article 51":
a system is classified as "based on its provider's general-purpose AI model with systemic risk" on the basis of a provision
    if the triage of the system continues
    and an operator is the provider of the general purpose AI model a model of the system
    and the model has systemic risk under the provision.

rule class_gpai with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(63) and Chapter V":
a system is classified as "based on its provider's general-purpose AI model" on the basis of "Article 3(63) and Chapter V"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and an operator is the provider of the general purpose AI model a model of the system
    and it is not the case that
        the model has systemic risk under a provision.

% ================================================================ step 4: general-purpose AI models (Chapter V)
% A general-purpose AI model is "in play" when the provider of the system is
% also the provider of the model it is built on. A system that only
% integrates another company's model through an interface makes its provider
% a downstream provider, not a model provider.
rule gpai_provider with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(3) and Article 3(63)":
an operator is the provider of the general purpose AI model a model of a system
    if the system is based on the general purpose AI model the model
    and the operator places the general purpose AI model the model on the market
    and the operator is a provider of the system.

rule systemic_risk_presumption with provenance the AI Act at "Article 51(1), point (a), and Article 51(2)",
        confer "presumed to have high impact capabilities ... when the cumulative amount of computation used for its training measured in floating point operations is greater than 10^25":
a model has systemic risk under "Article 51(1), point (a), and Article 51(2)"
    if the cumulative training computation of the model is a number N floating point operations
    and N > 10000000000000000000000000.

rule systemic_risk_designation with provenance the AI Act at "Article 51(1), point (b), and Article 52(4)":
a model has systemic risk under "Article 51(1), point (b)"
    if the Commission designates the model as a general purpose AI model with systemic risk.

general purpose AI is in play for a system
    if an operator is the provider of the general purpose AI model a model of the system.

% ================================================================ step 7: the obligation map
section remedy is:

% ---- AI literacy
rule literacy with provenance the AI Act at "Article 4 and Article 113, third paragraph, point (a)",
        confer "This obligation does not require providers or deployers to guarantee any specific level of AI literacy of any individual":
"Article 4 (AI literacy of staff)" applies to an operator for a system from 2025-02-02
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a provider of the system.

rule literacy_deployer with provenance the AI Act at "Article 4 and Article 113, third paragraph, point (a)":
"Article 4 (AI literacy of staff)" applies to an operator for a system from 2025-02-02
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a deployer of the system.

% ---- high-risk systems: the date of application of Chapter III
rule chapter_iii_annex_iii with provenance the AI Act at "Article 113, third paragraph, point (c)(i)":
the date of application of Chapter III to a system is 2027-12-02
    if the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of a provision.

rule chapter_iii_annex_i with provenance the AI Act at "Article 113, third paragraph, point (c)(ii)":
the date of application of Chapter III to a system is 2028-08-02
    if the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 1 because of an act
    and it is not the case that
        the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of a provision.

rule high_risk_provider with provenance the AI Act at "Chapter III, Sections 1 to 3, and Article 16":
"Chapter III, Sections 1 to 3 (requirements for high-risk AI systems, provider obligations of Article 16)" applies to an operator for a system from a date
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a provider of the system
    and the date of application of Chapter III to the system is the date
    and it is not the case that
        the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 1 because of an act
        and the act is in Section "B" of Annex I
        and it is not the case that
            the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of a provision.

rule high_risk_section_b with provenance the AI Act at "Article 2(2)",
        confer "only Article 6(1), Article 60a and Articles 102 to 112 shall apply":
"Article 6(1), Article 60a and Articles 102 to 112 only, through the sectoral legislation of Annex I, Section B" applies to an operator for a system from a date
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a provider of the system
    and the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 1 because of an act
    and the act is in Section "B" of Annex I
    and the date of application of Chapter III to the system is the date.

rule high_risk_deployer with provenance the AI Act at "Article 26 and Article 113, third paragraph, point (c)":
"Article 26 (obligations of deployers of high-risk AI systems)" applies to an operator for a system from a date
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a deployer of the system
    and the system is high risk
    and the date of application of Chapter III to the system is the date.

% ---- systems already on the market (Article 111(2), as replaced in 2026)
rule legacy with provenance the AI Act at "Article 111(2), first sentence",
        confer "this Regulation shall apply to operators of high-risk AI systems ... that have been placed on the market or put into service before the date of application of Chapter III ... only if, as from that date, those systems are subject to significant changes in their designs":
a system is subject to Chapter III only after a significant change in its design from a date
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the date of application of Chapter III to the system is the date
    and the system was first placed on the market or put into service on a first date
    and the first date is before the date.

rule legacy_public with provenance the AI Act at "Article 111(2), second sentence":
"Article 111(2): compliance of a high-risk system intended for use by public authorities" applies to an operator for a system from 2030-08-02
    if the system is subject to Chapter III only after a significant change in its design from a date
    and the operator is an operator of the system
    and an authority is a public authority
    and the authority is a deployer of the system.

% ---- the Article 6(3) derogation: documentation and registration
rule derogation_documentation with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(4) and Article 49(2)",
        confer "A provider who considers that an AI system referred to in Annex III is not high-risk shall document its assessment before that system is placed on the market or put into service":
"Article 6(4) and Article 49(2) (document the assessment that the system is not high-risk, and register it)" applies to an operator for a system from 2027-12-02
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is not high risk under Article 6 paragraph 3 because of a provision
    and it is not the case that
        the system is high risk
    and the operator is a provider of the system.

% ---- the fundamental rights impact assessment (Article 27)
rule fria_public with provenance the AI Act at "Article 27(1)":
an operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of a provision
    and it is not the case that
        the provision is "Annex III, point 2"
    and the operator is a deployer of the system
    and the operator is a body governed by public law.

rule fria_public_services with provenance the AI Act at "Article 27(1)":
an operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of a provision
    and it is not the case that
        the provision is "Annex III, point 2"
    and the operator is a deployer of the system
    and the operator is a private entity providing public services.

rule fria_credit with provenance the AI Act at "Article 27(1)",
        confer "and deployers of high-risk AI systems referred to in points 5 (b) and (c) of Annex III":
an operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of "Annex III, point 5(b)"
    and the operator is a deployer of the system.

rule fria_insurance with provenance the AI Act at "Article 27(1)":
an operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for a system
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of "Annex III, point 5(c)"
    and the operator is a deployer of the system.

rule fria_date with provenance the AI Act at "Article 27(1) and Article 113, third paragraph, point (c)(i)":
"Article 27 (fundamental rights impact assessment before the first use)" applies to an operator for a system from 2027-12-02
    if the operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for the system.

rule fria_dpia with provenance the AI Act at "Article 27(4)":
an operator may reuse its data protection impact assessment in the fundamental rights impact assessment for a system
    if the operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for the system
    and the operator has a data protection impact assessment for the system.

the fundamental rights impact assessment for a system is "required"
    if an operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for the system.

the fundamental rights impact assessment for a system is "needs analysis: the status of a deployer is not stated"
    if the triage of the system has the gap "whether each deployer is a body governed by public law or a private entity providing public services (Article 27(1))".

the fundamental rights impact assessment for a system is "not required"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and it is not the case that
        an operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for the system
    and it is not the case that
        the triage of the system has the gap "whether each deployer is a body governed by public law or a private entity providing public services (Article 27(1))".

% ---- general-purpose AI models
rule gpai_date with provenance the AI Act at "Article 113, third paragraph, point (b), and Article 111(3)":
the date of application of Chapter V to a model is a date
    if the model was placed on the market on a placing date
    and the placing date is before 2025-08-02
    and the date is 2027-08-02
    otherwise the date is 2025-08-02.

rule gpai_documentation with provenance the AI Act at "Article 53(1), points (a) and (b), and Article 53(2)",
        confer "The obligations set out in paragraph 1, points (a) and (b), shall not apply to providers of AI models that are released under a free and open-source licence ... This exception shall not apply to general-purpose AI models with systemic risks":
"Article 53(1), points (a) and (b) (technical documentation, information for downstream providers)" applies to an operator for a system from a date
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is the provider of the general purpose AI model a model of the system
    and the date of application of Chapter V to the model is the date
    and it is not the case that
        the model is released under a free and open source licence with its parameters publicly available
        and it is not the case that
            the model has systemic risk under a provision.

rule gpai_copyright with provenance the AI Act at "Article 53(1), points (c) and (d)":
"Article 53(1), points (c) and (d) (copyright policy, summary of the training content)" applies to an operator for a system from a date
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is the provider of the general purpose AI model a model of the system
    and the date of application of Chapter V to the model is the date.

rule gpai_systemic with provenance the AI Act at "Article 55(1)":
"Article 55 (obligations for models with systemic risk: evaluation, adversarial testing, incidents, cybersecurity)" applies to an operator for a system from a date
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is the provider of the general purpose AI model a model of the system
    and the model has systemic risk under a provision
    and the date of application of Chapter V to the model is the date.

% ---- transparency (Article 50), from the general date of application
rule transparency_interaction with provenance the AI Act at "Article 50(1) and Article 113, second paragraph":
"Article 50(1) (inform persons that they interact with an AI system)" applies to an operator for a system from 2026-08-02
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a provider of the system
    and the system is intended to interact directly with natural persons
    and it is not the case that
        it is obvious to the natural persons concerned that they interact with the system
    and it is not the case that
        the system is authorised by law to detect or prevent or investigate or prosecute criminal offences.

rule transparency_marking with provenance the AI Act at "Article 50(2)":
"Article 50(2) (machine-readable marking of synthetic content)" applies to an operator for a system from a date
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a provider of the system
    and the system generates synthetic a kind content
    and it is not the case that
        the system only performs an assistive function for standard editing or does not substantially alter the input data
    and it is not the case that
        the system is authorised by law to detect or prevent or investigate or prosecute criminal offences
    and the date of application of Article 50 paragraph 2 to the system is the date.

rule marking_date with provenance the AI Act at "Article 111(4)",
        confer "Providers of AI systems ... generating synthetic audio, image, video or text content, that have been placed on the market before 2 August 2026 shall take the necessary steps in order to comply with Article 50(2) by 2 December 2026":
the date of application of Article 50 paragraph 2 to a system is a date
    if the system was first placed on the market or put into service on a first date
    and the first date is before 2026-08-02
    and the date is 2026-12-02
    otherwise the date is 2026-08-02.

rule transparency_emotion with provenance the AI Act at "Article 50(3)":
"Article 50(3) (inform persons exposed to emotion recognition or biometric categorisation)" applies to an operator for a system from 2026-08-02
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a deployer of the system
    and the system is an emotion recognition system or a biometric categorisation system
    and it is not the case that
        the system is authorised by law to detect or prevent or investigate or prosecute criminal offences.

rule transparency_deep_fake with provenance the AI Act at "Article 50(4), first subparagraph":
"Article 50(4), first subparagraph (disclose deep fakes)" applies to an operator for a system from 2026-08-02
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a deployer of the system
    and the system generates or manipulates image or audio or video content that is a deep fake
    and it is not the case that
        the system is authorised by law to detect or prevent or investigate or prosecute criminal offences.

rule transparency_public_text with provenance the AI Act at "Article 50(4), second subparagraph":
"Article 50(4), second subparagraph (disclose text generated to inform the public)" applies to an operator for a system from 2026-08-02
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the operator is a deployer of the system
    and the operator publishes text generated by the system to inform the public on matters of public interest
    and it is not the case that
        the text that the operator publishes from the system has undergone human review or editorial control under its editorial responsibility.

% ---- the status of each obligation on the triage date
an obligation already applies to an operator for a system
    if the obligation applies to the operator for the system from a date
    and the system is triaged on a triage date
    and the date is before or equal to the triage date.

an obligation applies to an operator for a system only from a date that is in a number days
    if the obligation applies to the operator for the system from the date
    and the system is triaged on a triage date
    and the triage date is before the date
    and the date is the number days after the triage date.

the prohibition of a provision already applies to an operator for a system
    if the operator is subject to the prohibition of the provision for the system
    and the prohibition of the provision applies from a date
    and the system is triaged on a triage date
    and the date is before or equal to the triage date.

the prohibition of a provision applies to an operator for a system only from a date that is in a number days
    if the operator is subject to the prohibition of the provision for the system
    and the prohibition of the provision applies from the date
    and the system is triaged on a triage date
    and the triage date is before the date
    and the date is the number days after the triage date.

% ================================================================ step 8: escalation and gaps
section escalation is:

rule escalate_prohibited with provenance the Mike workflow at "Human gate", confer "suspected prohibited practice (Article 5)":
a system goes to a human for a full analysis because "a prohibited practice is suspected (Article 5)"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and the system involves a prohibited practice.

rule escalate_high_risk with provenance the Mike workflow at "Human gate", confer "high-risk":
a system goes to a human for a full analysis because "the system is high-risk (Article 6)"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk.

rule escalate_gpai with provenance the Mike workflow at "Human gate", confer "GPAI":
a system goes to a human for a full analysis because "general-purpose AI is in play (Chapter V)"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and general purpose AI is in play for the system.

section gaps is:

rule gap_machine with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
the triage of a system has the gap "whether the system is a machine-based system (Article 3(1))"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and it is not the case that
        the system is not an AI system
    and it is not the case that
        the system is a machine based system.

rule gap_autonomy with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
the triage of a system has the gap "whether the system is designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy (Article 3(1))"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and it is not the case that
        the system is not an AI system
    and it is not the case that
        the system is designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy.

rule gap_inference with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
the triage of a system has the gap "whether the system infers from its input how to generate outputs (Article 3(1))"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and it is not the case that
        the system is not an AI system
    and it is not the case that
        the system infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs.

rule gap_influence with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(1)":
the triage of a system has the gap "whether the outputs of the system can influence physical or virtual environments (Article 3(1))"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and it is not the case that
        the system is not an AI system
    and it is not the case that
        the outputs of the system can influence physical or virtual environments.

rule gap_profiling with provenance the AI Act at "Article 6(3), third subparagraph":
the triage of a system has the gap "whether the system performs profiling of natural persons (Article 6(3), third subparagraph)"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is used for a use listed in a provision
    and it is not the case that
        the system performs profiling of natural persons
    and it is not the case that
        the system does not perform profiling of natural persons.

rule gap_market_date_marking with provenance the AI Act at "Article 111(4)":
the triage of a system has the gap "when the system was first placed on the market or put into service (Article 111(4))"
    if "Article 50(2) (machine-readable marking of synthetic content)" applies to an operator for the system from a date
    and it is not the case that
        the system was first placed on the market or put into service on a first date.

rule gap_market_date_legacy with provenance the AI Act at "Article 111(2)":
the triage of a system has the gap "when the system was first placed on the market or put into service (Article 111(2))"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk
    and it is not the case that
        the system was first placed on the market or put into service on a first date.

rule gap_computation with provenance the AI Act at "Article 51(2)":
the triage of a system has the gap "the cumulative training computation of the general-purpose AI model (Article 51(2))"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and an operator is the provider of the general purpose AI model a model of the system
    and it is not the case that
        the cumulative training computation of the model is a number floating point operations
    and it is not the case that
        the Commission designates the model as a general purpose AI model with systemic risk.

rule gap_deployer_status with provenance the AI Act at "Article 27(1)":
the triage of a system has the gap "whether each deployer is a body governed by public law or a private entity providing public services (Article 27(1))"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and the system is high risk under Article 6 paragraph 2 because of a provision
    and an operator is a deployer of the system
    and it is not the case that
        the operator must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment for the system
    and it is not the case that
        the operator is a private entity that does not provide public services.

rule gap_material with provenance the AI Act at "Article 5(1a), point (a)":
the triage of a system has the gap "whether the system can generate intimate imagery of identifiable persons or child sexual abuse material without adequate safeguards (Article 5(1a))"
    if the system is triaged on a date
    and the system is an AI system
    and the system generates synthetic a kind content
    and it is not the case that
        the kind is text
    and a material is the material of a provision
    and it is not the case that
        the system involves the practice the material prohibited by the provision
    and it is not the case that
        the system has adequate safeguards that reliably prevent generating the material.

rule gap_role with provenance the AI Act at "Article 3(3) to (8)":
the triage of a system has the gap "who is the provider of the system (Article 3(3))"
    if the triage of the system continues
    and it is not the case that
        an operator is a provider of the system.

% ================================================================ the scenarios
scenario hr_screener is, as stated in the TalentSift vendor pack:
    TalentSift is triaged on 2026-11-03.
    HireLogic BV places TalentSift on the market under its own name.
    TalentSift was first placed on the market or put into service on 2025-06-01.
    TalentSift is a machine based system.
    TalentSift is designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy.
    TalentSift infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs.
    the outputs of TalentSift can influence physical or virtual environments.
    TalentSift is intended to be used for the recruitment or selection of natural persons.
    TalentSift performs profiling of natural persons.
    TalentSift is intended to perform a preparatory task to an assessment relevant for the use cases of Annex III,
        according to HireLogic BV, confer "only performs a preparatory task".
    Acme Logistics GmbH uses TalentSift under its own authority, as stated in the Acme data protection impact assessment.
    Acme Logistics GmbH is a private entity that does not provide public services, as stated in the Acme data protection impact assessment.
    classification expects answers ["TalentSift is classified as an AI system on the basis of Article 3(1)", "TalentSift is classified as high-risk under Article 6(2) on the basis of Annex III, point 4(a)"].
    roles expects answers ["HireLogic BV has the role provider for TalentSift", "Acme Logistics GmbH has the role deployer for TalentSift"].
    in_force expects answers ["Article 4 (AI literacy of staff) already applies to HireLogic BV for TalentSift", "Article 4 (AI literacy of staff) already applies to Acme Logistics GmbH for TalentSift"].
    later expects answers ["Chapter III, Sections 1 to 3 (requirements for high-risk AI systems, provider obligations of Article 16) applies to HireLogic BV for TalentSift only from 2027-12-02 that is in 394 days", "Article 26 (obligations of deployers of high-risk AI systems) applies to Acme Logistics GmbH for TalentSift only from 2027-12-02 that is in 394 days"].
    legacy expects answers ["TalentSift is subject to Chapter III only after a significant change in its design from 2027-12-02"].
    fria expects answers ["the fundamental rights impact assessment for TalentSift is not required"].
    escalation expects answers ["TalentSift goes to a human for a full analysis because the system is high-risk (Article 6)"].
    gaps expects answers [].
    flip_hr expects changes [["add: TalentSift does not infer how to generate its outputs"], ["add: TalentSift is not a machine based system"], ["remove: TalentSift is a machine based system"], ["remove: TalentSift is designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy"], ["remove: TalentSift infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs"], ["remove: the outputs of TalentSift can influence physical or virtual environments"], ["remove: TalentSift is intended to be used for the recruitment or selection of natural persons"]].

